  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday morning through next Monday)\nIssued at 234 PM EDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nThe extended periods begin dry with high pressure centered over the \nCarolinas and North GA. Near-seasonal/slightly-above-seasonal \ntemperatures, thanks to a mid-upper-level pattern that favors \nsubsidence and high pressure across the Southeast. This ridge begins \nto break down Friday allowing thew old frontal boundary across \ncentral FL to begin moving back north.  \n\nSaturday through Monday:\n\nOur eyes will be on the Gulf over the coming days regarding the \ndevelopment and evolution of an area of low pressure currently in \nthe southwestern Gulf. At this time, the National Hurricane Center \n(NHC) has an almost 100% chance of tropical formation over the next \n48 hours. Features that will impact the track of this system \ninclude: The high high pressure along the Eastern Seaboard (which \ncould induce cold air damming or the \"wedge\" across GA and a \nlongwave trough across the western CONUS. Steering flow between \nthese two features should bring whatever comes of the system \nnorthward into the northern Gulf and somewhere along the Gulf Coast \nregion. As far as potential impacts to north and central Georgia -- \nit is much too early to say. Right now the biggest impact will be \ninches of precipitation across the region but exactly where an d how \nmuch is still up in the air. Current QPF predictions from WPC \nshowing around 1 to 3 inches across the CWA. We should also see some \nbreezy conditions but like the precipitation, just how strong will \nbe highly dependent on the eventual track of the storm. At this time, \nsustained winds up to 15 mph and gusts up to 25-30 mph are on the \ntable. Flash flooding and/or rises on rivers, creeks, and streams \nwould also be possible. \n\nThe current models do show this tropical low pressure system making \nlandfall this weekend and making its way northward into the \nbeginning of next week. The differences in timing, location, and \nintensity vary greatly but they all do show it weakening \nsubstantially with a drier airmass moving into the region by \nMon/Tue. As we get into next week this should bring cooler daytime \nhigh temperatures in the 70s to lower 80s.",
